WEBVTT

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.664
On the fourteenth of November 1963,

00:00:02.664 --> 00:00:07.160
fishermen off southern Iceland saw dark columns rising from the sea.

00:00:07.160 --> 00:00:09.011
A new island was being born.

00:00:10.111 --> 00:00:15.579
Surtsey lies about thirty-two kilometres off Iceland's south coast, in the Westman Islands.

00:00:16.679 --> 00:00:21.530
This photograph was taken from an aircraft on the thirtieth of November 1963,

00:00:21.530 --> 00:00:26.443
sixteen days after the eruption began: a black cone of ash, wreathed in steam.

00:00:27.543 --> 00:00:32.109
The eruption began on the seafloor, about one hundred and thirty metres down.

00:00:32.109 --> 00:00:35.726
At first it was explosive, because seawater flooded the vent:

00:00:35.726 --> 00:00:38.394
magma meeting water shatters into glassy ash,

00:00:38.394 --> 00:00:42.012
thrown out in jets and in columns up to nine kilometres high.

00:00:42.012 --> 00:00:44.739
Geologists now call this a Surtseyan eruption.

00:00:44.739 --> 00:00:47.704
In April 1964 the growing cone sealed out the sea,

00:00:47.704 --> 00:00:50.194
and lava flows began to armour the island.

00:00:51.294 --> 00:00:56.199
Hot water then circulated through the loose ash and cemented it into hard rock called

00:00:56.199 --> 00:00:58.565
palagonite tuff, within years to decades.

00:00:58.565 --> 00:01:03.181
Drilling in 2017 found the inside of the island still above one hundred degrees,

00:01:03.181 --> 00:01:06.701
with a funnel-shaped vent cutting down into the old seafloor.

00:01:07.801 --> 00:01:09.813
The eruption ended in June 1967.

00:01:09.813 --> 00:01:13.209
Surtsey had reached two point seven square kilometres.

00:01:13.209 --> 00:01:16.605
Its neighbours, Heimaey and the other Westman Islands,

00:01:16.605 --> 00:01:20.630
are the products of older eruptions in the same volcanic system.
